WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.060
What is the role of perfectionism
in language learning?

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.610
That's my subject today, and
I want to look at it from

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.930
four different points of view.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.489
Why would we want to pursue perfection?

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.989
Where is it more important
to pursue perfection?

00:00:13.159 --> 00:00:16.979
When, at what stage in our
language learning should we try to

00:00:16.979 --> 00:00:18.739
pursue improvement or perfection?

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.379
And fourth, how do we go about
improving or pursuing that

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:24.939
elusive goal of perfection?

00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:29.739
Upfront, we are unlikely
to achieve perfection.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.060
So pure perfectionism is

00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:35.695
out of place and counterproductive
in language learning.

00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:40.535
However, the desire to constantly
improve is an important part of

00:00:40.595 --> 00:00:42.475
motivation in language learning.

00:00:42.615 --> 00:00:46.445
And I think that's a major reason
why the pursuit of perfection,

00:00:46.475 --> 00:00:49.654
even if we don't achieve it, has
its place in language learning.

00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:51.845
It's a natural thing
to want to get better.

00:00:51.955 --> 00:00:55.985
So you're working for a company in
English or German or Japanese, and

00:00:55.985 --> 00:00:57.715
you're sending out correspondence.

00:00:57.745 --> 00:00:59.324
You can't make too many mistakes.

00:00:59.324 --> 00:01:03.095
It reflects badly on you and on
the company you're working for.

00:01:03.414 --> 00:01:09.355
Certainly preparing for tests is an area
where we try to be better because language

00:01:09.384 --> 00:01:13.455
instruction teaches us to be afraid
of mistakes, even though in reality.

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:16.440
People who are more adventurous
in their use of the language

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.530
may make more mistakes, but
they're going to achieve more.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.240
They're going to communicate better.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.060
So it's a bit of
a double-edged sword.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:27.009
If we're too hung up about making
mistakes, we won't progress in

00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:31.639
the language, but obviously tests,
professional situations, and a natural

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.760
desire to want to improve are all reasons.

00:01:34.085 --> 00:01:35.585
To pursue perfection.

00:01:35.745 --> 00:01:39.545
So then the question is, where do
we want to work towards perfection?

00:01:39.725 --> 00:01:43.595
The most, the area that most people
seem to be concerned about when it

00:01:43.595 --> 00:01:48.314
comes to being overly self critical
or wanting to be better or wanting

00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:50.145
to be perfect is pronunciation.

00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:55.375
And to me, that is the least important,
as long as you're able to be understood.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.449
Then that's good enough.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.350
There is a very low likelihood
that language learners will

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.339
end up sounding like a native.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.660
You can maybe try to improve, but
you're going to be less than perfect.

00:02:07.669 --> 00:02:08.850
You should accept it.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.879
So perfectionism in pronunciation
to me is the least important.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.489
At the other end of the spectrum,
things like spelling are very

00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:17.260
important because if you're writing.

00:02:17.535 --> 00:02:20.685
And particularly if you're working
for a company or you're writing

00:02:20.785 --> 00:02:25.345
in university, spelling mistakes
reflect very badly on the writer.

00:02:25.405 --> 00:02:29.594
Now, fortunately today, much of
our writing is on a computer, so

00:02:29.595 --> 00:02:31.555
we are able to check the spelling.

00:02:31.815 --> 00:02:34.165
However, a lot of mistakes slip through.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.840
And so I think it's important to focus
on spelling as an area where we, if not

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.950
perfect, we need to be very, very good.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.340
Now, in between those two extremes,
pronunciation and spelling, we

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.920
have things like grammar and
the related idea of word usage.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:54.250
To me, word usage, natural word usage is
more important than details of grammar.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:58.600
But both of those, I think we
can be a little more permissive.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:03.480
Even in business communication, as long
as the meaning is clear, I think there

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.070
is less of a demand for perfection
than say, in the case of spelling,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.580
the same holds for punctuation.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.020
So at what stage in our language
learning should we be more concerned

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.620
about our accuracy or our perfectionism?

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.300
Very definitely, I think we should
be more focused on being perfect or

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.780
less inaccurate at a later stage.

00:03:24.185 --> 00:03:31.215
Upfront, any pressure on the learner to
produce correctly is counterproductive.

00:03:31.305 --> 00:03:35.664
Most people give up because they're
frustrated, because they feel they're

00:03:35.664 --> 00:03:37.595
not doing well or they're not learning.

00:03:37.885 --> 00:03:41.204
And so any additional pressure at
the early stage is counterproductive.

00:03:41.524 --> 00:03:43.135
I do not believe that fossilized.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.720
The brain is naturally developing this
model of the language, anticipating the

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.090
language, helping us create utterances
in the language, drawing on things that

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.060
it has stored in its long term memory.

00:03:56.289 --> 00:03:58.250
All of this takes time to accumulate.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.109
So at a later stage in the language,
when we want to really improve certain

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.290
aspects where we know we're, we're
not doing as well as we would like.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:07.230
It's never too late.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.630
I don't believe that if a
person is motivated to improve.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.910
That the mistakes they make at the early
stage are going to be with them and that

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.550
they'll never be able to improve them.

00:04:16.659 --> 00:04:17.709
I simply don't believe that.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:22.220
So less emphasis on getting things
right, mastering the basics, all

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.810
of these things up front, more
latitude for mistakes early on.

00:04:26.155 --> 00:04:30.185
Encourage the learner, encourage
yourself, and then later on, when

00:04:30.185 --> 00:04:32.865
you're a little more comfortable in
the language, you can go back in and

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.145
sort of clean up the, uh, rough edges.

00:04:35.345 --> 00:04:38.645
So then if we say that it's
legitimate to want to constantly

00:04:38.645 --> 00:04:41.604
improve, but it's highly unlikely
that we're ever going to be perfect.

00:04:41.605 --> 00:04:43.435
So perfectionism per se is out.

00:04:43.564 --> 00:04:44.995
How do we continue improving?

00:04:45.144 --> 00:04:48.875
And here, I think it's obvious that
it takes time to learn a language.

00:04:48.925 --> 00:04:52.075
So therefore the way to work
towards perfection is simply to

00:04:52.075 --> 00:04:54.695
keep going, to enjoy the language.

00:04:54.885 --> 00:04:58.045
To not worry about how we're
doing, not worry about mistakes.

00:04:58.045 --> 00:05:02.555
In fact, we learn from our mistakes to
forget and relearn and forget and relearn.

00:05:02.555 --> 00:05:06.095
That's how things are then
anchored in our long term memory.

00:05:06.365 --> 00:05:07.645
And that's how we end up with.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.400
Words and phrases that
we can comfortably use.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.290
My advice is don't worry
about being perfect.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:18.410
Try to get in the flow of the experience
of discovering a new language.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:23.069
And I think you'll be surprised at how
you continue to progress towards, if not

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.559
perfection, at least constant improvement.

00:05:26.079 --> 00:05:29.350
And I will leave you with two
videos on related subjects.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:31.400
One that talks about perfectionism.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.290
And another one that talks
about the importance of making

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.980
mistakes in language learning.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.240
Thank you for listening.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:37.940
Bye for now.

